---
import projectsData from '../data/projects_details.yaml';
const judo = projectsData.projects.find((p: any) => p.slug === 'judo-clipper');
const touchline = projectsData.projects.find((p: any) => p.slug === 'touchline');
const labels: Record<string, string> = { 'Visit website': 'Live app', 'Research repo': 'Model research' };
---
<section id="projects" class="work-section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-heading"><span class="section-number">01</span><h2>Selected work</h2><span class="section-aside">Models, systems &amp; products</span></div>
    <div class="work-grid">
      <article class="work-card flagship" id="judo-clipper">
        <div class="project-media"><div class="flagship-photo"><img src="/images/judo_clipper_project_icon.png" alt="Two judo competitors with tracked pose skeletons" width="631" height="346" loading="lazy" /></div><span class="media-caption">01 / Two-player pose tracking</span></div>
        <div class="work-body">
          <div class="work-intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">Applied machine learning / independent project</p>
          <h3>Judo Clipper</h3>
          <p>Turns full judo matches into short clips of likely throw attempts, helping athletes and coaches find moments to review without searching through the whole recording.</p>
          <p>I trained the system using footage I labelled by hand and built the service that processes uploaded videos. It follows both competitors’ movements rather than relying on clothing colour, so it can handle opponents wearing matching kit.</p>
          </div>
          <dl class="metrics"><div><dt>2,163</dt><dd>Hand-labelled<br />7-second clips</dd></div><div><dt>0.761</dt><dd>Attempt F1<br />Held-out test set</dd></div><div><dt>30 min</dt><dd>Maximum<br />video length</dd></div></dl>
          <p class="stack"><strong>Stack /</strong> Python, PyTorch, YOLO11x-pose, ByteTrack, FastAPI, Modal, AWS S3, FFmpeg, pytest (554 tests)</p>
          <p class="transparency">The ML pipeline and backend are my own work. The frontend was built with substantial generative AI assistance.</p>
          <div class="card-actions">{judo.links.map((link: any) => <a class:list={['small-btn', { primary: link.primary }]} href={link.url} target={link.url.startsWith('http') ? '_blank' : undefined} rel={link.url.startsWith('http') ? 'noopener' : undefined}>{labels[link.label] ?? link.label}</a>)}</div>
        </div>
      </article>
      <div class="supporting-projects">
      <article class="work-card touchline-card">
        <div class="project-media"><img src="/images/touchline-preview.webp" alt="Touchline showing Chelsea vs Swansea City with the automatic director and commentary captions" width="1200" height="597" loading="lazy" /><span class="media-caption">02 / Automated football director</span></div>
        <div class="work-body">
          <p class="eyebrow">HackTheBurgh / hackathon prototype</p><h3>Touchline</h3>
          <p>Explores automatically switching between simultaneous football broadcasts to show the most interesting match. Ranks seven streams using pose-based features, a temporal model and a heuristic switching policy. The demo runs on prerecorded matches.</p>
          <dl class="metrics touchline-metrics">
            <div><dt>7</dt><dd>Concurrent streams</dd></div>
            <div class="match-selection"><dt>Automated match selection</dt><dd>Pose estimation · Temporal modelling · Heuristic switching</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="stack"><strong>Stack /</strong> {touchline.tech.join(', ')}</p>
          <div class="card-actions">{touchline.links.map((link: any) => <a class:list={['small-btn', { primary: link.primary }]} href={link.url} target={link.url.startsWith('http') ? '_blank' : undefined} rel={link.url.startsWith('http') ? 'noopener' : undefined}>{link.primary ? 'Live demo' : link.label}</a>)}</div>
        </div>
      </article>
      <article class="work-card hub-card">
        <div class="project-media"><img src="/images/edinburghai-hub-preview.webp" alt="EdinburghAI Hub homepage with workshop resources, upcoming events and a society workshop photograph" width="1200" height="598" loading="lazy" /><span class="media-caption">03 / EdinburghAI member platform</span></div>
        <div class="work-body">
          <p class="eyebrow">03 / Live member platform</p><h3>EdinburghAI Hub</h3>
          <p>A live platform for EdinburghAI with an early user base, helping students discover events, connect with other members and participate in the society. Supabase powers member accounts and authentication. I defined the features and requirements, built and launched it using AI-assisted development, and iterated with committee feedback.</p>
          <p class="product-note">Supabase · Member accounts &amp; authentication · AI-assisted development</p>
          <div class="card-actions"><a class="small-btn primary" href="https://edinburghai-hub.osheaconboy.chatgpt.site/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Live site</a></div>
        </div>
      </article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <a class="text-link writing-link" href="/blog">All project write-ups</a>
  </div>
</section>
